-- erwartet: C001
-- **665 -- the form that was written to EMBED a quote, and it embeds a space.**
--
-- The third of `D6`'s assembler-only shapes, and the one that carries a finding of its own.
-- Two string literals side by side were read as Gabbro's doubling form for a quote inside a
-- name -- the shape a C or Rust programmer reaches for. **It is not that.**
-- `parse.rs::erwarte_text` joins adjacent literals **with a space**, so `"a""b"` is one name
-- reading `a b`, and no quote is embedded anywhere.
--
-- Measured 2026-09-02, before the repair:
--
--     static uint64_t x __attribute__((section("a b"))) … = 0;
--     as: Error: junk at end of line, first unrecognized character is `b`
--
-- The assembler's complaint says it exactly: it read `.section a`, then found a second word.
-- `messung/ERZEUGERDEFEKTE.md` §6 had recorded the assembler's answer without the cause; the
-- refusal names it now, and it names the space.
--
-- > **The written claim this refutes.** The note beside `emit.rs::kommentartext` had ruled
-- > this channel closed in so many words: a Gabbro string cannot contain a quote, so nothing
-- > can escape out of a `section` attribute. That is true of the QUOTE and says nothing about
-- > the character the join puts there instead. *A closed channel is a claim about every
-- > character, and it had been checked against one.*

module gift::two_string_literals_join_with_a_space {

static mut x : u64 = 0 section "a""b";

}
